"""Independently written explanations; no textbook questions or wording."""
def example(title, question, solution, verification):
    return dict(title=title, question=question, solution=solution, verification=verification)
def lesson(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):
    return dict(key=key,id='fp1_roots_'+key.replace('-','_'),title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method=method,mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples)
lessons=[
lesson('coefficients','Roots and coefficients of quadratic, cubic and quartic equations',
 'Read the coefficients as information about all the roots, without solving the equation.',
 'Use root–coefficient relations, including multiplicities, to recover missing coefficients and root patterns.',
 r'<p>You need to expand products, factorise quadratics and solve simultaneous equations. Revisit <a href="/courses/maths4u-9709-p1/topics/orig_quadratics_factorisation">quadratic equations</a> or <a href="/courses/maths4u-9709-p1/topics/orig_quadratics_simultaneous">simultaneous equations</a> if needed.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Why the signs alternate</h3><p>For a polynomial of degree \(n\) with leading coefficient \(a\ne0\), write \(P(x)=a\prod_{j=1}^{n}(x-r_j)\), counting roots with multiplicity. To expand the product, choose either \(x\) or a negative root from each factor. Choosing one root gives a minus sign, two give a plus sign, and three give a minus sign.</p>
 <p>Let \(e_1\) be the sum of the roots, \(e_2\) the sum of their pair products, and so on. Each combination of root positions is counted once.</p>
 <h3>Quadratic</h3>\[ax^2+bx+c=0:\qquad e_1=-\frac ba,\quad e_2=\frac ca.\]
 <h3>Cubic</h3>\[ax^3+bx^2+cx+d=0:\qquad e_1=-\frac ba,\quad e_2=\frac ca,\quad e_3=-\frac da.\]
 <h3>Quartic</h3>\[ax^4+bx^3+cx^2+dx+e=0:\qquad e_1=-\frac ba,\quad e_2=\frac ca,\quad e_3=-\frac da,\quad e_4=\frac ea.\]
 <p>A quartic has six pair products in \(e_2\) and four triple products in \(e_3\). For example, \(e_2=r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_1r_4+r_2r_3+r_2r_4+r_3r_4\).</p>
 <h3>Reconstruct the equation</h3><p>A monic cubic is \(x^3-e_1x^2+e_2x-e_3=0\); a monic quartic is \(x^4-e_1x^3+e_2x^2-e_3x+e_4=0\). Multiplying the whole equation by a non-zero constant preserves its roots.</p>
 <h3>Multiplicity and reality</h3><p>Roots \(u,u,v\) give \(e_1=2u+v\), \(e_2=u^2+2uv\), \(e_3=u^2v\). The relations also hold for non-real roots. They do not, by themselves, prove that all roots are real: use the restrictions, factorisation or a discriminant when reality matters.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Arrange descending powers and insert zero coefficients for missing terms.</li><li>Identify the degree and leading coefficient.</li><li>Write the required symmetric sums with alternating signs.</li><li>Translate the given conditions into equations and find all candidates.</li><li>Check restrictions and expand the reconstructed polynomial.</li></ol>',
 ['Forgetting to divide by the leading coefficient.','Using the cubic constant term as the product without changing its sign.','Counting a pair twice, or dropping a repeated root position.','Rejecting parameter candidates before checking the stated restrictions.'],
 [example('Read a non-monic quadratic',r'<p>The roots of \(6x^2-5x-4=0\) are \(u,v\). Find their sum and product.</p>',r'\[u+v=-\frac{-5}{6}=\frac56,\qquad uv=\frac{-4}{6}=-\frac23.\]<p>No individual root calculation is needed. The negative product also shows that this quadratic has two real roots of opposite signs.</p>','E-C1'),
 example('Three sums from a cubic',r'<p>For \(2x^3-7x^2-3x+9=0\), find \(e_1,e_2,e_3\) and the monic equation.</p>',r'\[e_1=\frac72,\qquad e_2=-\frac32,\qquad e_3=-\frac92.\]\[x^3-\frac72x^2-\frac32x+\frac92=0.\]<p>The final constant is \(-e_3\). Multiplying by 2 recovers the given equation.</p>','E-C2'),
 example('Do not skip the triple products',r'<p>For \(3x^4+6x^3-4x^2+5x-2=0\), find the four elementary symmetric sums.</p>',r'\[e_1=-2,\quad e_2=-\frac43,\quad e_3=-\frac53,\quad e_4=-\frac23.\]<p>The coefficient of \(x\) determines the sum of all four triple products; the constant determines the product of all four roots.</p>','E-C3'),
 example('A repeated-root reconstruction',r'<p>A monic cubic has roots \(u,u,v\), where \(v&gt;u&gt;0\). Their sum is 5 and their pair-product sum is 7. Find the cubic.</p>',r'<p>From \(2u+v=5\), put \(v=5-2u\). Then</p>\[u^2+2u(5-2u)=7\ \Longrightarrow\ 3u^2-10u+7=0.\]<p>Thus \(u=1\) or \(7/3\). The second gives \(v=1/3&lt;u\), contrary to the question. Hence \(u=1,v=3\), with product 3.</p>\[x^3-5x^2+7x-3=(x-1)^2(x-3)=0.\]','E-C4')]),
lesson('symmetric-sums','Symmetric functions of roots and power sums',
 'Reduce complicated root expressions to a small set of symmetric sums.',
 'Evaluate symmetric expressions and derive a root-equation recurrence for higher power sums.',
 r'<p>First study <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_roots_coefficients">roots and coefficients</a>. You need square and cube expansions and exact fraction arithmetic.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Squares and cubes</h3><p>Put \(S_k=\sum_{j=1}^{n}r_j^k\), so \(S_0=n\). Expanding the square of the sum counts every pair twice:</p>\[S_2=e_1^2-2e_2.\]<p>Collecting terms in the cube expansion gives</p>\[S_3=e_1S_2-e_2S_1+3e_3=e_1^3-3e_1e_2+3e_3.\]
 <h3>Reciprocal sums need non-zero roots</h3><p>For a cubic, combining fractions gives</p>\[\sum\frac1{r_j}=\frac{e_2}{e_3},\qquad \sum_{i&lt;j}\frac1{r_ir_j}=\frac{e_1}{e_3}.\]<p>For a quartic these are \(e_3/e_4\) and \(e_2/e_4\). Apply the square-sum identity to the reciprocal roots for a reciprocal square sum. Check the constant term before dividing.</p>
 <h3>Derive a recurrence from the root equation</h3><p>If \(ar^3+br^2+cr+d=0\) at each root, multiply by \(r^k\) and sum over the three root positions:</p>\[aS_{k+3}+bS_{k+2}+cS_{k+1}+dS_k=0\quad(k\ge0).\]<p>At \(k=0\), the last term is \(3d\), not \(d\). For a quartic, the corresponding relation is</p>\[aS_{k+4}+bS_{k+3}+cS_{k+2}+dS_{k+1}+eS_k=0.\]<p>Find the initial sums first. These formulae use non-negative powers; negative powers require non-zero roots. Asking for \(S_8\) does not change the degree of the original polynomial.</p>
 <h3>Check whether data are independent</h3><p>A later power sum may follow automatically from earlier data and add no new information. If a coefficient remains free after substitution, do not invent a value for it. A negative sum of squares is possible when the roots are not all real.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>List the elementary sums from the coefficients.</li><li>Expand the requested expression or express it in those sums.</li><li>For higher powers, multiply the root equation by a suitable power and sum.</li><li>Include \(S_0=n\), work exactly and check denominators.</li></ol>',
 ['Confusing the pair-product sum with the square sum.','Forgetting to sum the constant once for each root position.','Using reciprocal identities when a root might be zero.','Assuming that the roots must all be real.'],
 [example('Squares and cubes without solving',r'<p>For the roots of \(x^3-3x^2+2x+4=0\), find \(S_2,S_3\).</p>',r'<p>Here \(e_1=3,e_2=2,e_3=-4\).</p>\[S_2=3^2-2(2)=5,\qquad S_3=3(5)-2(3)+3(-4)=-3.\]','E-S1'),
 example('A reciprocal square sum',r'<p>Use the same cubic to evaluate \(\sum r_i^{-2}\).</p>',r'<p>The product is \(-4\ne0\). The reciprocal sum is \(-1/2\), and the reciprocal pair-product sum is \(-3/4\).</p>\[\sum r_i^{-2}=\left(-\frac12\right)^2-2\left(-\frac34\right)=\frac74.\]','E-S2'),
 example('A sixth power from a recurrence',r'<p>For \(x^3-x-2=0\), derive a power-sum recurrence and find \(S_6\).</p>',r'<p>Each root satisfies \(r^3=r+2\). Multiplication by \(r^k\) and summation gives</p>\[S_{k+3}=S_{k+1}+2S_k\quad(k\ge0).\]<p>Start with \(S_0=3,S_1=0,S_2=2\). Thus \(S_3=6,S_4=2,S_5=10\), and \(S_6=S_4+2S_3=14\).</p>','E-S3'),
 example('Reconstruct and then divide',r'<p>Three non-zero roots have \(S_1=1,S_2=9,S_3=-5\). Find their monic cubic and reciprocal sum.</p>',r'\[e_1=1,\qquad e_2=\frac{1-9}{2}=-4.\]<p>The cube identity gives \(-5=9+4+3e_3\), so \(e_3=-6\). Hence</p>\[x^3-x^2-4x+6=0,\qquad \sum\frac1r=\frac{-4}{-6}=\frac23.\]<p>The non-zero constant confirms that reciprocals are defined.</p>','E-S4')]),
lesson('transformations','Transforming equations by substitution',
 'Build the new equation by expressing the old variable in terms of the new one.',
 'Construct equations for shifted, scaled, reciprocal and squared roots, accounting for excluded values.',
 r'<p>Use <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_roots_coefficients">root–coefficient relations</a> and <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_roots_symmetric_sums">symmetric sums</a>. Practise expanding brackets and clearing rational denominators first.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Choose the correct direction</h3><p>If the new root is \(y=mx+h\), with \(m\ne0\), substitute \(x=(y-h)/m\) in the old equation. For degree \(n\), multiply by \(m^n\) to clear constant denominators. Adding \(h\) to roots therefore means substituting \(x=y-h\), not \(y+h\).</p>
 <h3>Reciprocals and squares</h3><p>For reciprocal roots, check \(P(0)\ne0\), substitute \(x=1/y\) and form \(y^nP(1/y)\). For squared roots, separate odd and even powers, or form \(P(x)P(-x)\) and replace \(x^2\) by \(y\), adjusting the overall sign if necessary.</p><p>Different old roots can have the same square. Retain their multiplicities in the new polynomial.</p>
 <h3>A rational transformation has two restrictions</h3><p>For \(y=(mx+h)/(px+q)\), require \(mq-hp\ne0\). Check that no old root makes \(px+q=0\). Rearranging gives</p>\[x=\frac{h-qy}{py-m}.\]<p>After clearing a denominator, test the excluded new value \(py-m=0\) in the final polynomial. Explain why no root has been lost or added.</p>
 <h3>Simplify high powers first</h3><p>At a root of \(x^3+ax+b=0\), the expression \(x^3\) equals \(-ax-b\). A transformation involving cubes can therefore become a linear substitution. The root equation is often more useful than a direct expansion.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Write the desired new root \(y=T(x)\).</li><li>Simplify \(T\) using the original root equation where possible.</li><li>Find the inverse relation or eliminate the old variable; state restrictions.</li><li>Substitute, clear denominators, expand and remove only non-zero common factors.</li><li>Check the degree, multiplicities and excluded values.</li></ol>',
 ['Using the forward expression where the inverse substitution is needed.','Dividing by a root without proving it is non-zero.','Dropping repeated transformed root values.','Assuming that squaring cannot introduce extra roots.'],
 [example('Translate all three roots',r'<p>Find a monic equation for roots two more than those of \(x^3-2x^2-3x+1=0\).</p>',r'<p>Put \(y=x+2\), hence \(x=y-2\).</p>\[(y-2)^3-2(y-2)^2-3(y-2)+1=0\]\[\Longrightarrow\quad y^3-8y^2+17y-9=0.\]<p>The new sum is \(2+3\times2=8\), matching the coefficient.</p>','E-T1'),
 example('Reverse coefficients with a reason',r'<p>Find an equation for the reciprocal roots of \(x^3+2x^2-3x+4=0\).</p>',r'<p>The constant 4 is non-zero. Substitute \(x=1/y\) and multiply by \(y^3\):</p>\[1+2y-3y^2+4y^3=0.\]<p>Thus \(4y^3-3y^2+2y+1=0\). Its constant is 1, so \(y=0\) has not been introduced.</p>','E-T2'),
 example('Square the roots and check the reverse step',r'<p>Find a monic cubic for the squared roots of \(x^3-2x+1=0\).</p>',r'<p>Each old root satisfies \(x(x^2-2)=-1\). Put \(y=x^2\) and square:</p>\[y(y-2)^2=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad y^3-4y^2+4y-1=0.\]<p>Conversely, \(y=2\) is not a root. Set \(x=-1/(y-2)\); the new equation gives \(x^2=y\) and recovers the original equation. This justifies the transformation.</p>','E-T3'),
 example('A fractional transformation and its pole',r'<p>Transform the roots of \(x^3-x^2+2x-3=0\) using \(y=(x+2)/(x-1)\).</p>',r'<p>Since \(P(1)=-1\), no old root is excluded. The inverse is \(x=(y+2)/(y-1)\), with \(y\ne1\). After multiplication by \((y-1)^3\),</p>\[(y+2)^3-(y+2)^2(y-1)+2(y+2)(y-1)^2-3(y-1)^3=0.\]<p>Expansion gives \(y^3-12y^2+3y-19=0\). Its value at \(y=1\) is \(-27\), so clearing the denominator has not added the excluded value.</p>','E-T4')]),
lesson('mixed','Mixed problems',
 'Choose the right relation, justify restrictions and connect several methods.',
 'Combine root relations, substitutions and reality conditions in multi-step polynomial problems.',
 r'<p>Complete <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_roots_coefficients">coefficients</a>, <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_roots_symmetric_sums">symmetric sums</a> and <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-fp1/topics/fp1_roots_transformations">transformations</a>. A few optional written problems require 3-by-3 determinants or complex conjugate roots; attempt those after the relevant prerequisite.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Pick the shortest route</h3><p>Use coefficients for elementary sums, the root equation for high powers and an inverse substitution for a transformed equation. Solving a quartic explicitly is unnecessary if only a symmetric sum is requested.</p>
 <h3>Translate root patterns into equations</h3><p>Write an arithmetic progression as \(a-d,a,a+d\), a non-zero geometric pattern as \(a/r,a,ar\), and two double roots as a squared quadratic. State restrictions: \(d&gt;0\) gives distinct values; \(r=1\) repeats a value.</p>
 <h3>Reciprocal quartics</h3><p>When coefficients read the same forwards and backwards, divide by \(x^2\) and put \(t=x+1/x\). Then \(x^2+x^{-2}=t^2-2\). For real non-zero \(x\),</p>\[t\in(-\infty,-2]\cup[2,\infty).\]<p>Indeed, \(x^2-tx+1=0\) has discriminant \(t^2-4\). Each \(|t|&gt;2\) gives two distinct real roots; \(t=\pm2\) gives a repeated root.</p>
 <h3>Centre a quartic</h3><p>For \(ax^4+bx^3+\cdots=0\), substitute \(x=y-b/(4a)\) to remove the cubic term. If the roots come in pairs with the same pair-sum, this shift can remove the linear term too. Solve an even quartic as a quadratic in \(y^2\).</p>
 <h3>Reality must be justified</h3><p>If every root is real, a sum of real squares is non-negative. A negative value proves that not all roots are real; it does not identify the non-real roots. For real coefficients, non-real roots occur in conjugate pairs.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Record which roots must be real, distinct or non-zero.</li><li>Use root patterns or choose a useful substitution.</li><li>Solve the coefficient conditions, keeping all algebraic candidates.</li><li>Check discriminants, signs and denominators.</li><li>Give the complete result, with multiplicities and reasons for rejecting candidates.</li></ol>',
 ['Assuming that every parameter candidate satisfies the required reality condition.',r'Accepting \(|t|&lt;2\) when \(t=x+1/x\) must come from real x.','Listing distinct values but omitting multiplicities.','Applying a real-square inequality to roots not known to be real.'],
 [example('Three equally spaced roots',r'<p>A monic cubic has roots \(a-2,a,a+2\) and root sum 9. Find its equation.</p>',r'<p>Since \(3a=9\), the roots are \(1,3,5\). Their pair-product sum is \(23\), and their product is 15.</p>\[x^3-9x^2+23x-15=(x-1)(x-3)(x-5)=0.\]','E-M1'),
 example('Count the real roots after substitution',r'<p>Show that \(x^4-4x^3+3x^2-4x+1=0\) has exactly two real roots.</p>',r'<p>Zero is not a root. Divide by \(x^2\), set \(t=x+1/x\) and obtain</p>\[t^2-4t+1=0,\qquad t=2\pm\sqrt3.\]<p>Only \(2+\sqrt3&gt;2\) is admissible for real x. Its quadratic has positive discriminant, giving exactly two real roots:</p>\[x=\frac{2+\sqrt3\pm\sqrt{3+4\sqrt3}}2.\]','E-M2'),
 example('Centre a quartic before solving it',r'<p>The quartic \(x^4-8x^3+Ax^2+Bx-15=0\) has roots in two pairs, each pair summing to 4. Their square sum is 36. Find A, B and all roots.</p>',r'<p>The sum is 8, so \(36=64-2A\), giving \(A=14\). Put \(x=y+2\). Each pair becomes opposite values, so the new polynomial is even. Its linear coefficient is \(B-8\), giving \(B=8\).</p>\[P(y+2)=y^4-10y^2+9=(y^2-1)(y^2-9).\]<p>Thus \(y=\pm1,\pm3\), and the original roots are \(\{-1,1,3,5\}\). Their product is \(-15\), as required.</p>','E-M3'),
 example('A family with no all-real case',r'<p>Prove that \(x^4+x^3+cx^2-x-1=0\) cannot have four real roots for any real c.</p>',r'<p>The product is \(-1\), so reciprocals are defined. The original square sum is \(1-2c\); the reciprocal square sum is \(1+2c\). Hence</p>\[\sum_{j=1}^4\left(r_j-\frac1{r_j}\right)^2=(1-2c)+(1+2c)-8=-6.\]<p>If all roots were real, every term would be non-negative. This contradiction rules out four real roots. The real coefficients imply that at least two roots are non-real.</p>','E-M4')])
]
